++# Mirror Process Authority and Lifecycle Diagnosis (Gemini)
++
++**Date**: 2026-09-13  
++**Worker**: Gemini  
++**Workspace**: `C:\Users\hello\AppData\Local\AgentControlRoom\worktrees\uoink-library\213914a5-528\gemini`  
++**Reference Brief**: `docs/library/MIRROR-TREE08-REPAIR-BRIEF-2026-09-13.md`  
++**Boundary Rules**: Diagnostic review and synthetic inert-process regression probes only. No product edits, existing-test edits, real process manipulation, kernel gate acquisition, model execution, live index access, port 5179 listeners, new fetch, paid API calls, commits, pushes, or subagents.
++
++---
++
++## 1. Assignment Scope and Authority Boundaries
++
++Tree08 produced 51 mirror failures across 2,729 test cases (proof record: `proof/ryan-final-partitioned-08-2026-09-13/SHA256.json`). The first failure was `test_live_worker_lease_blocks_a_second_mirror` in `tests/test_phase4_av5m3_isolation.py` at initial export, followed by cascading failures reaching `WaitForSingleObject` and `destination exclusion unavailable`.
++
++Astra is independently investigating cancellation and owner lifetime. Gemini owns the bounded security and lifecycle assignment: inspect the process authority mechanisms in [library_mirror.py](file:///C:/Users/hello/AppData/Local/AgentControlRoom/worktrees/uoink-library/213914a5-528/gemini/library_mirror.py), specifically:
++1. Authority used by `owned_pids`, `physical_liveness`, and termination.
++2. Windows Toolhelp parent PID persistence (`th32ParentProcessID`) outliving parent process identity.
++3. Whether a recycled or orphan parent PID causes a foreign child to be adopted, retained, or killed.
++4. Whether a confirmed process exit can be forgotten on a later failed query.
++
++All diagnostic assertions in this report were verified with synthetic regression probes in [tests/test_gemini_synthetic_process_authority.py](file:///C:/Users/hello/AppData/Local/AgentControlRoom/worktrees/uoink-library/213914a5-528/gemini/tests/test_gemini_synthetic_process_authority.py) executed via the guarded native verifier.
++
++---
++
++## 2. Source-Level Authority Analysis
++
++### 2.1 Toolhelp Snapshot Mechanics and Orphan Ingestion
++
++In [library_mirror.py](file:///C:/Users/hello/AppData/Local/AgentControlRoom\worktrees\uoink-library\213914a5-528\gemini/library_mirror.py#L529-L561):
++```python
++529: def _windows_process_children(parent_pid: int | None) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:
++...
++540:     snapshot = k32.CreateToolhelp32Snapshot(_TH32CS_SNAPPROCESS, 0)
++...
++549:         while True:
++550:             if int(entry.th32ParentProcessID) == int(parent_pid):
++551:                 out.append({
++552:                     "pid": int(entry.th32ProcessID),
++553:                     "exe": str(entry.szExeFile or ""),
++554:                     "ppid": int(entry.th32ParentProcessID),
++555:                 })
++556:             if not Process32NextW(snapshot, ctypes.byref(entry)):
++557:                 break
++558:         return out
++```
++
++In the Windows NT kernel, `th32ParentProcessID` reflects the creator PID assigned at process initialization (`InheritedFromUniqueProcessId`). Windows does not update, re-parent, or clear this field when the parent process exits. When an old process terminates, any surviving children retain that creator PID as their parent PID indefinitely.
++
++Because Windows reuses PIDs rapidly, when a new process is spawned and receives a recycled PID, any preexisting orphan processes whose original creator had that same PID match `entry.th32ParentProcessID == parent_pid`.
++
++### 2.2 Unchecked Adoption in `owned_pids()`
++
++In [library_mirror.py](file:///C:/Users/hello/AppData/Local/AgentControlRoom\worktrees\uoink-library\213914a5-528\gemini/library_mirror.py#L1427-L1456):
++```python
++1427:     def owned_pids(self) -> list[int]:
++1428:         pids: list[int] = []
++1429:         seen: set[int] = set()
++1430: 
++1431:         def add(pid: int | None, exe: str = "", created: int | None = None) -> None:
